#!/bin/sh
# Grow the root filesystem to fill whatever card the image was flashed
# onto. The image ships with a small, fixed-size root partition (see
# image/config/scoreboard.yaml); most of a real SD card is otherwise
# unused, with no room for logs to grow.
#
# Two-phase across a reboot -- the same technique raspi-config's
# do_expand_rootfs has used for years (see RPi-Distro/raspi-config). It is
# NOT reinvented here: growing a partition table entry while its
# filesystem is mounted only rewrites the table (sector 0), never the
# data, since the start sector is never moved -- but the running kernel
# has already cached the old, smaller table in memory and only ever
# re-reads it fresh at boot, so resize2fs cannot act on the new size
# until after a reboot.
#
# Safe by construction:
#   - refuses unless the root partition is the LAST partition on the disk
#     (growing one that has another partition after it would overwrite
#     that partition's data)
#   - the start sector is passed back to sfdisk explicitly unchanged, on
#     top of sfdisk's own refusal to write an invalid/overlapping layout
#   - idempotent and safe to run on every boot: sfdisk validates and
#     commits its new layout atomically (no partial writes to recover
#     from), and resize2fs is a documented no-op when there is nothing
#     left to grow
#   - MBR only (matches this image's layout); GPT would additionally need
#     its backup header relocated, which this script does not attempt
#
# Run by nhl-scoreboard-grow-rootfs.service, gated so it stops trying
# once $DONE_MARKER exists.
set -eu

# Overridable so tests/test_grow_rootfs.py can run this against a tmp
# directory instead of real system state.
STATE_DIR="${NHL_SCOREBOARD_STATE_DIR:-/var/lib/nhl-scoreboard}"
GROWN_MARKER="$STATE_DIR/rootfs-partition-grown"
DONE_MARKER="$STATE_DIR/rootfs-grown"

log() {
    logger -t nhl-scoreboard-grow-rootfs "$1"
    echo "$1"
}

if [ -f "$DONE_MARKER" ]; then
    exit 0
fi
mkdir -p "$STATE_DIR"

ROOT_PART=$(findmnt / -o source -n)
ROOT_DEV="/dev/$(lsblk -no pkname "$ROOT_PART")"
PART_NUM=$(echo "$ROOT_PART" | grep -o '[0-9]*$')

if [ -f "$GROWN_MARKER" ]; then
    # Stage 2: we grew the partition table on a previous boot and have
    # since rebooted, so the kernel now sees the larger partition.
    log "Growing filesystem on $ROOT_PART to fill its partition"
    if resize2fs "$ROOT_PART"; then
        touch "$DONE_MARKER"
        rm -f "$GROWN_MARKER"
        log "Root filesystem grown; done"
        exit 0
    fi
    log "resize2fs failed; will retry next boot"
    exit 1
fi

# Stage 1: grow the partition table entry, then reboot so the kernel
# re-reads it fresh.
LAST_PART_NUM=$(parted "$ROOT_DEV" -ms unit s p | tail -n 1 | cut -f 1 -d:)
if [ "$LAST_PART_NUM" != "$PART_NUM" ]; then
    log "$ROOT_PART (partition $PART_NUM) is not the last partition on $ROOT_DEV (that's $LAST_PART_NUM); refusing to grow it"
    touch "$DONE_MARKER"
    exit 0
fi

PART_START=$(parted "$ROOT_DEV" -ms unit s p | grep "^${PART_NUM}:" | cut -f 2 -d: | sed 's/[^0-9]//g')
if [ -z "$PART_START" ]; then
    log "Could not determine the start of $ROOT_PART; refusing to grow it"
    touch "$DONE_MARKER"
    exit 0
fi

log "Growing partition $PART_NUM on $ROOT_DEV (start sector $PART_START unchanged, size -> max)"
if ! printf '%s,+\n' "$PART_START" | sfdisk --no-reread -N "$PART_NUM" "$ROOT_DEV"; then
    log "sfdisk failed; partition table left untouched, will retry next boot"
    exit 1
fi

touch "$GROWN_MARKER"
log "Partition table grown; rebooting so the kernel picks it up"
systemctl --no-block reboot
